Output: ['<|begin_of_text|>Below is an instruction that describes a task, paired with an input that provides further context. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.\n\n### Instruction:\nCreate an edited version of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria that incorporates the logical operators [AND], [OR], [NOT].\nApply these rules for inserting the logical operators:\n- Always write an [OR] before an "or", "and/or", "and / or" in the text.\n- Always write an [OR] after a comma "," and after "/" in the text.\n- Always write an [AND] before words like "with", "who", "in addition", "plus", "and", "but", "that", "despite", "having".\n- Always write a [NOT] before "no", "not", "none",  "don\'t", "free", "prevent", "Inability", "lack", "impossible", "off", "without", "unable", "naive", "excluded", "absence"\n\nGeneral rules:\n- The operators [OR], [AND], [NOT] should be written before the words like or, and, not.\n- An [AND], [OR], [NOT] must not come before a line segment (\\n).\n- Please carefully read through the given criteria text, then output only an edited version with the logical operators incorporated, enclosed in <EDITED_CRITERIA> tags.\n- Do not include any other explanations or output.\n\n\n### Input:\nParticipation in the 4 weeks preceding inclusion or planned participation during the present trial period in another clinical trial investigating a vaccine, drug, medical device, or medical procedure. \nReceipt of any vaccine in the 4 weeks preceding each trial vaccination or planned receipt of any vaccine in the 4 weeks following each trial vaccination, except for: \n(i) influenza vaccination, which may be received at least 2 weeks before study vaccines. \n(ii) measles (M) or measles, mumps, rubella (MMR) routine vaccination, which can be administered concomitantly with the first dose of study vaccine as per routine immunization schedule \n(iii) for subjects enrolled at Indian sites: oral poliomyelitis vaccine (OPV) received during National Immunization Days (NIDs) and supplementary immunization activity days (SIADs) \nPrevious vaccination against meningococcal disease with either the study vaccine or another meningococcal vaccine \nReceipt of immune globulins, blood or blood-derived products in the past 3 months \nKnown or suspected congenital or acquired immunodeficiency; or receipt of immunosuppressive therapy, such as anti-cancer chemotherapy or radiation therapy, within the preceding 6 months; or long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy (prednisone or equivalent for more than 2 consecutive weeks within the past 3 months) \nHistory of meningococcal diseases, confirmed either clinically, serologically, or microbiologically \nAt high risk, in the opinion of the Investigator, for meningococcal disease during the trial \nKnown or suspected systemic hypersensitivity to any of the vaccine components, or history of a life-threatening reaction to the vaccine used in the trial or to a vaccine containing any of the same substances \nKnown thrombocytopenia, contraindicating intramuscular vaccination \nBleeding disorder, or receipt of anticoagulants in the 3 weeks preceding inclusion, contraindicating intramuscular vaccination \nIn an emergency setting, or hospitalized involuntarily \nChronic illness that, in the opinion of the investigator, is at a stage where it might interfere with trial conduct or completion \nFor subjects enrolled at Indian sites: Moderate or severe acute illness/infection (according to investigator judgment) on the day of vaccination or febrile illness (temperature ≥ 38.0°C). \nFor subjects enrolled at Russian sites: Acute disease of any severity on the day of vaccination or febrile illness (axillary temperature ≥ 37.0°C). \nA prospective subject should not be included in the study until the condition has resolved or the febrile event has subsided. \nReceipt of oral or injectable antibiotic therapy within 72 hours prior to the first blood draw \nIdentified as a natural or adopted child of the Investigator or employee with direct involvement in the proposed study \nPersonal history of Guillain-Barré Syndrome.       \n    \n\n\n### Response:\nParticipation in the 4 weeks preceding inclusion [OR] or planned participation during the present trial period in another clinical trial investigating a vaccine, [OR] drug, [OR] medical device, [OR] or medical procedure. \nReceipt of any vaccine in the 4 weeks preceding each trial vaccination [OR] or planned receipt of any vaccine in the 4 weeks following each trial vaccination, except for: \n(i) influenza vaccination, which may be received at least 2 weeks before study vaccines. \n(ii) measles (M) [OR] or measles, mumps, [OR] rubella (MMR) routine vaccination, which can be administered concomitantly with the first dose of study vaccine as per routine immunization schedule \n(iii) for subjects enrolled at Indian sites: oral poliomyelitis vaccine (OPV) received during National Immunization Days (NIDs) [OR] and supplementary immunization activity days (SIADs) \nPrevious vaccination against meningococcal disease with either the study vaccine [OR] or another meningococcal vaccine \nReceipt of immune globulins, [OR] blood [OR] or blood-derived products in the past 3 months \nKnown [OR] or suspected congenital [OR] or acquired immunodeficiency; [OR] or receipt of immunosuppressive therapy, such as anti-cancer chemotherapy [OR] or radiation therapy, within the preceding 6 months; [OR] or long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy (prednisone [OR] or equivalent for more than 2 consecutive weeks within the past 3 months) \nHistory of meningococcal diseases, confirmed either clinically, [OR] serologically, [OR] or microbiologically \nAt high risk, in the opinion of the Investigator, for meningococcal disease during the trial \nKnown [OR] or suspected systemic hypersensitivity to any of the vaccine components, [OR] or history of a life-threatening reaction to the vaccine used in the trial [OR] or to a vaccine containing any of the same substances \n Known thrombocytopenia, contraindicating intramuscular vaccination \nBleeding disorder, [OR] or receipt of anticoagulants in the 3 weeks preceding inclusion, contraindicating intramuscular vaccination \nIn an emergency setting, [OR] or hospitalized involuntarily \nChronic illness that, in the opinion of the investigator, is at a stage where it might interfere with trial conduct [OR] or completion \nFor subjects enrolled at Indian sites: Moderate [OR] or severe acute illness/ [OR] infection (according to investigator judgment) on the day of vaccination [OR] or febrile illness (temperature ≥ 38.0°C). \nFor subjects enrolled at Russian sites: Acute disease of any severity on the day of vaccination [OR] or febrile illness (axillary temperature ≥ 37.0°C). \nA prospective subject should [NOT] not be included in the study until the condition has resolved [OR] or the febrile event has subsided. \nReceipt of oral [OR] or injectable antibiotic therapy within 72 hours prior to the first blood draw \nIdentified as a natural [OR] or adopted child of the Investigator or employee with direct involvement in the proposed study \nPersonal history of Guillain-Barré Syndrome.<|eot_id|>']
Response: Participation in the 4 weeks preceding inclusion [OR] or planned participation during the present trial period in another clinical trial investigating a vaccine, [OR] drug, [OR] medical device, [OR] or medical procedure. 
Receipt of any vaccine in the 4 weeks preceding each trial vaccination [OR] or planned receipt of any vaccine in the 4 weeks following each trial vaccination, except for: 
(i) influenza vaccination, which may be received at least 2 weeks before study vaccines. 
(ii) measles (M) [OR] or measles, mumps, [OR] rubella (MMR) routine vaccination, which can be administered concomitantly with the first dose of study vaccine as per routine immunization schedule 
(iii) for subjects enrolled at Indian sites: oral poliomyelitis vaccine (OPV) received during National Immunization Days (NIDs) [OR] and supplementary immunization activity days (SIADs) 
Previous vaccination against meningococcal disease with either the study vaccine [OR] or another meningococcal vaccine 
Receipt of immune globulins, [OR] blood [OR] or blood-derived products in the past 3 months 
Known [OR] or suspected congenital [OR] or acquired immunodeficiency; [OR] or receipt of immunosuppressive therapy, such as anti-cancer chemotherapy [OR] or radiation therapy, within the preceding 6 months; [OR] or long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy (prednisone [OR] or equivalent for more than 2 consecutive weeks within the past 3 months) 
History of meningococcal diseases, confirmed either clinically, [OR] serologically, [OR] or microbiologically 
At high risk, in the opinion of the Investigator, for meningococcal disease during the trial 
Known [OR] or suspected systemic hypersensitivity to any of the vaccine components, [OR] or history of a life-threatening reaction to the vaccine used in the trial [OR] or to a vaccine containing any of the same substances 
 Known thrombocytopenia, contraindicating intramuscular vaccination 
Bleeding disorder, [OR] or receipt of anticoagulants in the 3 weeks preceding inclusion, contraindicating intramuscular vaccination 
In an emergency setting, [OR] or hospitalized involuntarily 
Chronic illness that, in the opinion of the investigator, is at a stage where it might interfere with trial conduct [OR] or completion 
For subjects enrolled at Indian sites: Moderate [OR] or severe acute illness/ [OR] infection (according to investigator judgment) on the day of vaccination [OR] or febrile illness (temperature ≥ 38.0°C). 
For subjects enrolled at Russian sites: Acute disease of any severity on the day of vaccination [OR] or febrile illness (axillary temperature ≥ 37.0°C). 
A prospective subject should [NOT] not be included in the study until the condition has resolved [OR] or the febrile event has subsided. 
Receipt of oral [OR] or injectable antibiotic therapy within 72 hours prior to the first blood draw 
Identified as a natural [OR] or adopted child of the Investigator or employee with direct involvement in the proposed study 
Personal history of Guillain-Barré Syndrome.
